R e a d m e
-----------

Tags and names blocks on grids and subgrids.

Names use the root grid as an ID before the full stop. Each hinge, rotor, or piston leading to a subgrid is added before the block name. For example:

    Miner.Hinge Drill

Text after a block's standard name is retained while its automatic number is removed:

    LCD Panel 3 System Status Display
    Miner.LCD Panel System Status Display

Programmable Blocks become `PB`, Timer Blocks become `Timer`, Event Controllers become `EC`, and trailing numbers are removed from standard block type names.

Antennas and beacons are named like any other block (`Flopsy.Antenna`), but their HUD text is set to the grid ID alone (`Flopsy`), so the marker on your HUD shows the ship, not the block. Laser antennas have no HUD text.

Blocks of the same type are not numbered, so several identical blocks share a name. Use `name_override` to tell them apart.

Setup
-----

1. Give the programmable block's root grid a name in the Info tab.
2. Load this script into a Programmable Block.
3. Run it with no argument. This tags and names the blocks once.

Commands
--------

- `run`: tag and name blocks. This is the default command.
- `tag`: only add tags.
- `name`: only rename blocks.
- `clear`: remove `[general]` tags while preserving other Custom Data.
- `config`: print the active settings.
- `dump`: write the discovered block list to the programmable block's Custom Data.
- `help`: list commands.

Text after a block's standard name is kept, so name tags used by other scripts, such as `[launchcontrol]` or `[fleet]`, survive renaming.

Configuration
-------------

Script settings use the `[tagger]` section of the programmable block. Settings with a default are added automatically, without comments:

    [tagger]
    include_connected_grids=false
    run_every_minutes=0
    watch_config=true

`include_connected_grids` also processes grids joined by connectors. It is disabled by default. Mechanical subgrids attached by hinges, rotors, or pistons are always processed.

Set `run_every_minutes` above zero to run automatically at that interval. Keep it at `0` to disable automatic runs. A changed interval restarts its countdown.

`watch_config` keeps the script ticking so edits to this section are picked up within a few seconds. Turn it off to leave the programmable block idle between commands; settings are then only reloaded when a command runs.

`grid_id` replaces the grid name as the ID for this block's own grid. Use it when the grid name is awkward, or on a non-English client where the script cannot recognise an unnamed grid. It only matters when you set it, so add the key yourself; the script does not write it.

Invalid values use defaults and report a warning. Malformed INI is preserved while defaults are used; repairing it resumes saving. Use one key=value per line.

Block name overrides
--------------------

Overrides are opt-in. Add the section yourself to a block's Custom Data:

    [tagger]
    name_override=Airlock

The override replaces the block's type name. The grid ID and any subgrid path are still applied, so the example above becomes:

    Miner.Airlock

If the override is on a hinge, rotor, or piston, it also becomes that subgrid's path name. For example, a hinge override of `Forearm` produces:

    Miner.Forearm Drill

Tags
----

Tags live under `[general]`:

    [general]
    tags=drill, tool

Every block is tagged with its type name, converted to snake_case with a leading `My` and a trailing `_block` removed:

    BatteryBlock         battery
    AirVent              air_vent
    LCDPanelsBlock       lcd_panels
    MyProgrammableBlock  programmable

Modded blocks follow the same rule, so they are always tagged. Categories are added on top, and are listed in the `tagCategories` table at the top of the script. That table is what makes a drill `tool` and a battery `power`.

Tags you add by hand are kept. Tags the script no longer produces are also kept, so run `clear` then `run` after editing the category table.

Moving grids also get position and facing tags, recalculated on every run:

- `position_bow`, `position_stern`, `position_port`, `position_starboard`, `position_upper`, `position_lower`, or `position_central`. Thirds are measured from the main cockpit, falling back to any ship controller.
- `facing_forward`, `facing_backward`, `facing_left`, `facing_right`, `facing_up`, `facing_down`, on thrusters, lights, doors, and cameras.

Static grids get no position or facing tags, and existing ones are removed when a ship becomes a station. A component with no cockpit or remote control has no orientation to measure against, so it gets no spatial tags either.

Existing valid INI Custom Data is preserved, and a block is only written to when its tags actually change. Blocks containing Custom Data which cannot be parsed as INI are skipped when tagging. Naming can still process them, but cannot read an override from invalid Custom Data.
